  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "The side lengths of a triangle are in the ratio $5:6:7$. The area of the triangle is $24\\sqrt{6}\\text{ cm}^2$. Find the side lengths of the triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$10\\text{ cm}$, $12\\text{ cm}$ and $14\\text{ cm}$.\n\nSet the sides to $5k$, $6k$ and $7k$ and use Heron’s formula to obtain $k=2$.",
      "worked": "Let the side lengths be $5k$, $6k$ and $7k\\text{ cm}$, where $k>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s&=\\frac{5k+6k+7k}{2}\\\\s&=9k\\\\A&=\\sqrt{9k(9k-5k)(9k-6k)(9k-7k)}\\\\A&=\\sqrt{9k\\times4k\\times3k\\times2k}\\\\A&=\\sqrt{216k^4}\\\\A&=6\\sqrt{6}\\,k^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nUsing the given area,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6\\sqrt{6}\\,k^2&=24\\sqrt{6}\\\\k^2&=4\\\\k&=2\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe side lengths are $5(2)=10\\text{ cm}$, $6(2)=12\\text{ cm}$ and $7(2)=14\\text{ cm}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
